@startuml timing
!include _theme.puml
' Event positions are ordinal, not measurements: this is the stale-worker unit-test scenario.
' Q/R/REC are expanded in the key to keep the short event intervals legible.
' Timing rows take their ink from the timingDiagram style, not from the colour
' skinparams the other diagram types use. The default style draws a dark-green
' line on a lavender band; this block pins the row and axis to black on white.
<style>
timingDiagram {
  LineColor #000000
  FontColor #000000
  concise {
    LineColor #000000
    BackgroundColor #FFFFFF
  }
  robust {
    LineColor #000000
    BackgroundColor #FFFFFF
  }
  clock {
    LineColor #000000
  }
  highlight {
    BackgroundColor #F2F2F2
  }
  arrow {
    LineColor #000000
  }
  constraintArrow {
    LineColor #000000
    FontColor #000000
  }
  timeline {
    LineColor #000000
    FontStyle plain
  }
}
</style>
concise "Embedding run" as R
concise "Work item" as I
concise "Lease owner" as L
scale 1 as 94 pixels
@0
R is Q
I is Q
L is "none"
@1
R is R
I is R
L is A
@2
R is REC
I is Q
L is "none"
@3
R is Q
@4
R is R
I is R
L is B
@5
legend bottom
  <b>Ordinal events, not elapsed time</b>
  Q = queued; R = running; REC = recoverable.
  0 Enqueue · 1 A claims · 2 Reap expired lease and stale run
  3 Admin resumes · 4 B claims · 5 Reject A's stale write
endlegend
@enduml
